%I A397639 #52 Aug 09 2026 22:05:25
%S A397639 0,1,0,-1,0,2,0,3,2,2,1,1,-1,3,-1,5,4,2,3,3,3,-1,0,2,1,3,0,2,1,4,-1,5,
%T A397639 6,1,4,5,4,5
%N A397639 a(n) = 2-adic valuation of n! minus the 2-adic valuation of the permanent of the n X n matrix with entries gcd(i,j).
%C A397639 a(n) <= 2*floor(log_2(n)) + 1, since the 2-adic valuation of A085244(n) is at least n - 2*floor(log_2(n)) - 2. See link.
%C A397639 Whether a(n) is unbounded above is open.
%C A397639 a(2^k+1) = 2k-4 holds for k = 2, 3, 4, 5. Whether it holds for larger k is not known; a(65) would require the permanent of a 65 X 65 matrix.
%C A397639 a(n) = -1 for n = 4, 13, 15, 22, 31, and a(n) >= 0 for all other n <= 38.
%H A397639 Vico Bonfioli, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21433867">Notes on d-matching polynomials, gcd-matrix permanents, and partition self-divisibility, with Lean 4 formalizations</a>, Zenodo, 2026.
%F A397639 a(n) = A011371(n) - A397298(n).
%e A397639 For n = 4 the matrix is [[1,1,1,1],[1,2,1,2],[1,1,3,1],[1,2,1,4]], with permanent 112 = 2^4 * 7, so its 2-adic valuation is 4. Also 4! = 24 = 2^3 * 3, of 2-adic valuation 3. Hence a(4) = 3 - 4 = -1.
%t A397639 a[n_] := IntegerExponent[n!, 2] - IntegerExponent[Permanent[Array[GCD, {n, n}]], 2]; Array[a, 15]
%o A397639 (PARI) a(n) = valuation(n!, 2) - valuation(matpermanent(matrix(n, n, i, j, gcd(i, j))), 2) \\ practical to about n = 20; the permanent is the bottleneck
%Y A397639 Cf. A007814, A011371, A085244, A397298.
%K A397639 sign,more
%O A397639 1,6
%A A397639 _Vico Bonfioli_, Aug 01 2026
